      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bridgeville earned the nickname “June Strawberry Capital of the Nation” after the railroad arrived in 1856, turning it into a top strawberry producer.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        10,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridgeville%2C_Delaware",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from a bridge built in 1730 over a Nanticoke River tributary, a landmark that still defines the community’s layout.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridgeville%2C_Delaware",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Bridgeville’s historic core includes the Sudler House, the old Library building, and a firehouse museum—all preserved by the Bridgeville Historical Society founded in 1976.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bridgevillehistoricalsocietyde.org/history/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Bridgeville’s roots trace back to a Native American village, with the land originally part of a grant to Lord Baltimore by the English Crown.",
